<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burgraviate of Nuremberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burgrave of Nuremberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Burgraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> was the office and territory of the burgraves who commanded the imperial castle of Nuremberg for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. From about 1192 it was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a>, who built up lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> around Ansbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>. Burgrave Frederick VI became elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1415, and in 1427 the family sold its castle at Nuremberg to the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg.jpg" alt="Burgraviate of Nuremberg" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TECHNOKRAT 14:55, 2 October 2008 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burgraves_of_Nuremberg_(Eigener_Entwurf_Version_2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> was probably founded around the turn of the 11th century. It is first mentioned in a document of 1050, as the site of an Imperial castle between eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and the Bavarian March of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordgau">Nordgau</a>, and it grew in importance through its place on key trade routes. King Conrad III established the burgraviate, with the first administration and courts over the Imperial territories around the town.</p><p>The first burgraves came from the Austrian House of Raabs. When their male line died out around 1190, the office passed to the last count's son-in-law. Frederick I, originally Frederick III, Count of Zollern, had married Sophia, daughter of Burgrave Conrad II, and became burgrave through this union, bringing the burgraviate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the late 12th century to the Interregnum (1254–1273) the power of the burgraves declined. The Staufen emperors gave most non-military powers to a castellan, and from 1173/74 the city's administration and municipal courts went to an Imperial mayor, the Reichsschultheiß. The castellan administered the Imperial lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, levied taxes, judged the highest cases of poaching and forestry, and protected churches and monasteries, even those of the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>. Relations between the burgraves and the castellan turned into open enmity.</p><p>In 1219 Emperor Frederick II granted the city the Great Letter of Freedom (Großen Freiheitsbrief), with town rights, Imperial immediacy, the right to mint coins and its own customs policy, which almost wholly removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> from the burgraves' control. The castellan's privileges passed to the city in the late 14th and early 15th centuries. Nuremberg became, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, one of the two great trading centres on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diets</a> and courts met at its castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Hohenzollern burgraves held the title for more than two centuries, among them Frederick III der Erber (1262–1297), John II der Erwerber (1332–1357) and Frederick V (1357–1397). Frederick V's sons divided the inheritance: John III was also Margrave of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a> from 1398, and Frederick VI, as Frederick I, became Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1415 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a> in 1417. The burgraviate was eventually partitioned to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burgraviate of Nuremberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1191–1192 Count Frederick III of Zollern, a follower of the Staufer emperors, acquired the office of <b>Burgrave of Nuremberg</b> through his marriage to Sophia of Raabs, ruling there as Frederick I. After his death, around 1200–1204, his sons Conrad and Frederick at first held the inheritance together…</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
